<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runner-upgrades"
      updated="August 21, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"what-moves", "Who upgrades what"},
        {"versions", "Find the versions you run"},
        {"compatibility", "Compatibility status"},
        {"plan", "Plan the rollout"},
        {"canary", "Upgrade one runner"},
        {"verify", "Verify the canary"},
        {"fleet", "Roll through the fleet"},
        {"rollback", "Return to a known-good release"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runner-upgrades">
        <:dek>
          Move a runner fleet to a new release. Start with one canary, verify the result, and
          keep the previous version ready for rollback.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To upgrade the workstation bridge instead, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/bridge-upgrades"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Upgrade the MCP bridge</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Owner or admin in the account — you will read runner versions and run history in the
          console.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Root or sudo on each runner host. The installer writes to
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/usr/local/bin</code>
          and the service unit.
        </:item>
        <:item>One non-critical runner to go first.</:item>
        <:item>
          The release you are currently on, written down. You need the exact tag to go back to it.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="what-moves">Who upgrades what</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Four parts upgrade separately — upgrading one never upgrades another.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Component</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Who upgrades it</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">How</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Control plane</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">We do</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Nothing for you to run. Shipped changes land in the <.link
                  navigate={~p"/changelog"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >changelog</.link>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Runner binary</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">You, per host</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Run <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar update</code>. It stops and restarts the service.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">emisar-mcp bridge</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">You, per workstation</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Re-run the installer — <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/bridge-upgrades"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Upgrade the MCP bridge</.link>. Cloud OAuth clients run no bridge.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Action packs</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">You, per runner</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Updated separately, with their own trust decision — <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="versions">Find the versions you run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The console shows the version that each runner last reported — the Runners list carries
        it per host. On the host, run:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># one machine-readable line: "emisar version X.Y.Z"</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar --version

<span class="text-zinc-500"># the same version plus Go, platform, and build metadata</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar version</.docs_code>
      <.docs_h2 id="compatibility">Compatibility status</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each version a runner reports is compared against two published numbers: the oldest
        release we still support, and the one we recommend you run.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Value</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Current setting</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Minimum</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>{Emisar.Compat.runner_minimum()}</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Recommended release</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>{Emisar.Compat.runner_target()}</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">outdated</strong>
            is above the minimum but behind the one we recommend. It keeps working. The console
            shows a version chip, and nothing else changes.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">unsupported</strong>
            is below the minimum. The runner is flagged on the Runners page. It still connects
            today, but that is not a promise. Upgrade it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The version is self-reported, so treat it as housekeeping, never a security control —
        what a runner may do is decided by your policies and pack trust, not by the version it
        claims.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Upgrade your runners and bridges in the same pass. For which versions work together, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/compatibility"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >compatibility and deprecation</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="plan">Plan the rollout</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300 list-decimal pl-6">
        <li>
          Read the
          <.link navigate={~p"/changelog"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">changelog</.link>
          for the releases between your current version and the target.
        </li>
        <li>
          Write down the version you run today. That tag is your way back. Nothing in the product
          remembers it for you.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check
          <a
            href={~p"/app/runs"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Runs</a>
          for work in flight on the hosts you are about to touch. Filter to
          running and sent, and let anything long-lived finish first.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pick one non-critical runner as the canary. Ideally in a group whose actions you can
          exercise safely.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="canary">Upgrade one runner</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner updates to the latest stable immutable release by default. Pin the release on
        the canary so every later host gets the exact same release.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># on the canary host</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar update --version &lt;target-version&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This command works only for a runner installed by <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">install.sh</code>, which records the paths it owns in a root-owned receipt. A copied
        binary, container image, or infrastructure-managed runner is refused — update that
        deployment at its source instead of letting the host drift from it.
      </p>
      <.docs_verify_download tarball="emisar-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz" checksums="SHA256SUMS" />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The update touches only the binary: <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">config.yaml</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.env</code>, the cached token, installed packs, the dispatch log, and the local
        audit journal all stay as they are. Before the service stops, it verifies the release and
        archive checksum (an authenticated
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">gh</code>
        CLI also checks the build attestation), stages the binary, checks its version, and
        confirms the new binary can read the current dispatch state.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="The service does stop">
        <p>
          Once the preflight passes, the installer stops the service, swaps the binary, and
          starts it again. A runner kills its children when it exits, so an action in flight on
          that host is interrupted — let running work finish first.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If any later step fails, the installer restores the previous binary, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.env</code>, and cached
        token, restarts the service if it was running before, and exits nonzero. Packs installed
        during the attempt stay installed.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">Verify the canary</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Six checks, in order — stop at the first one that fails.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># 1. the binary is the version you pinned</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar --version

<span class="text-zinc-500"># 2. the service came back and stayed up</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> systemctl status emisar

<span class="text-zinc-500"># 3. config, credential, packs, action binaries, and reachability</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar doctor

<span class="text-zinc-500"># 4. the packs and hashes this host will advertise</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack list</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then verify the console. In <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>, confirm that the canary is online with the new version and expected actions.
        Run one low-risk read-only action. Confirm that it succeeds and appears in <a
          href={~p"/app/audit"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Audit</a>. The audit row is what completes the check —
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">doctor</code>
        covers the host, and only a real run shows the whole path works.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="fleet">Roll through the fleet</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Repeat the same two steps — upgrade, then the full verification — in bounded batches,
        one runner group at a time. Halt when any of these happens:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The update exits non-zero. If the swap had started, that host is already back on its
            previous binary — read the output before you touch the next host.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar doctor</code>
            reports a failed check, or the runner does not come back online within its usual
            reconnect window.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The advertised action set changed in a way the release notes did not describe, or a
            pack lands as pending trust in <a
              href={~p"/app/packs"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Packs</a>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/production"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Go to production</.link>
        owns fleet shape and batch sizing.
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Manage the runner fleet</.link>
        owns groups, labels, and host-side removal.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="rollback">Return to a known-good release</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Going back is the same update command with the version you wrote down, followed by the
        same verification.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># runner — the release you were on before</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar update --version &lt;known-good-version&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Rollback has two limits. Only a finished, published release installs — a draft or one
        still being written is refused. And the older binary must be able to read the current
        dispatch state; if it cannot, the installer refuses and the current installation keeps
        running. Rollback works only within the supported release line — go back no further than
        the minimum above.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An upgrade or rollback never touches packs. If the problem is an action's
        behavior rather than the runner, the version to change is the pack's. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
